To write a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text, you can use the `markdown-c` library. This library provides a simple interface for converting markdown to HTML. Here's a basic implementation:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <markdown-c.h>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdownText) {
    markdown::Markdown md;
    std::string htmlText = md.convert(markdownText);
    return htmlText;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "# Hello, Markdown!\nThis is a test paragraph.";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);
    std::cout << "Markdown Text: " << markdownText << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML Text: " << htmlText << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

